Kenya.]

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:10.390
[And the Liberian Institute of the American
Foundation for Tropical Medicine]

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:15.399
Even today, in the age of the hydrogen bomb
and supersonic flight, much of Africa remains

00:01:15.399 --> 00:01:17.440
wild and primeval.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:22.290
In many parts of the continent, wild animals
such as the elephant, giraffe, hippopotamus,

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:26.610
and others still abound.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:30.750
Over large areas, man still lives a primitive
existence in much the same way as did his

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:31.750
forebears.

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:38.220
Civilization has left little imprint upon
him and he still carries on his daily occupation

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:53.250
in accordance with his ancient customs.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:58.230
In vast areas in Africa there are countless
persons suffering from many strange and exotic

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:01.310
diseases, some of which are found nowhere
else in the world.

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:05.490
Some of these diseases are swift killers,
while others leave their victims permanently

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:10.710
incapacitated until death finally intervenes.

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:14.650
These diseases greatly interfere with the
economic development of the tropical countries

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:19.940
and hamper the exportation of enormous reservoirs
of material resources so much needed by the

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:21.239
free world.

00:02:21.239 --> 00:02:26.090
Furthermore, there's a constant threat of
the spread beyond the tropics by means of

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:32.010
today's rapid transportation systems.

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:36.440
Research in the field of tropical medicine
is of more than academic or altruistic concern

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:40.970
to the people of the United States, because
our own economy is involved.

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:45.819
60 percent of our imports come from the tropical
countries, including many of the strategic

00:02:45.819 --> 00:02:48.860
materials which we lack.

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:52.769
Before one can hope to control or eliminate
these various diseases, it is necessary to

00:02:52.769 --> 00:02:58.840
study them in the areas in which they are
prevalent.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:02.810
To do this, scientists must make their way
into the heart of the endemic areas by whatever

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:04.120
means are available.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:09.370
Shown here is a National Institutes of Health
research team working as a part of the Point

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:18.749
Four program, leaving for a field survey in
the Liberian hinterland.

00:03:18.749 --> 00:03:22.590
Field investigations are especially important
in arriving at methods for the prevention

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:26.180
and control of tropical diseases.

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:32.370
Tropical medicine is not a static science
and the tropics cover vast areas of the globe.

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:37.400
Methods employed to combat a particular disease
in one part of the tropics may not be equally

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:40.170
applicable elsewhere.

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:44.900
All aspects of tropical medicine, however,
cannot be studied in the field but must be

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:47.319
investigated at special research centers.

00:03:47.319 --> 00:03:52.670
Not only in the tropics, but also in the more
advanced areas where specialized equipment

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:56.549
and qualified personnel are more readily available.

00:03:56.549 --> 00:04:02.170
This film will show how aspects of some of
the more important tropical diseases of Africa,

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:05.069
with special reference to conditions in Liberia,
West Africa.

00:04:05.069 --> 00:04:09.659
Together with some of the research work being
done not only in that area, but also at the

00:04:09.659 --> 00:04:17.410
National Institutes of Health of the United
States Public Health Service.

00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:22.840
Malaria is an acute, recurring, febrile disease
caused by protozoan parasites of the genus

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:29.370
plasmodium which are transmitted by an integral
part of their life cycle in certain Anopheline

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:30.370
mosquitoes.

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:35.860
This disease is one of the main obstacles
to successful settlement in many warm countries.

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:40.669
The part it plays in general world morbidity
and mortality is hard to judge, but it is

00:04:40.669 --> 00:04:46.190
thought to be responsible for more illness
and death per year than any other transmissable

00:04:46.190 --> 00:04:48.420
disease.

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:53.100
The prevalence of malaria parasites in a population
group can best be ascertained by means of

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:56.050
microscopic examination of thick blood smears.

00:04:56.050 --> 00:05:00.830
While the parasites are more abundant in the
blood of infants and children before the development

00:05:00.830 --> 00:05:07.021
of immunity, it is not uncommon in hyper-endemic
areas to demonstrate them in 80 to 90 percent

00:05:07.021 --> 00:05:10.310
of adults examined.

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:15.639
Thick blood smears are used primarily in surveys
and for case bindings because of high concentration

00:05:15.639 --> 00:05:18.800
of parasites provided by this technique.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:23.910
Thin blood smears are more suitable when exact
species determination is required or when

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:25.419
morphological studies are desired.

00:05:25.419 --> 00:05:30.650
In order to gain a complete picture of malaria
in any region, it is necessary to study the

00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:32.670
mosquitoes that transmit it.

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:37.030
Larvae and pupae of Anopheline mosquitoes
are sought in the field in order to locate

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:43.509
their breeding areas and to study the conditions
under which they multiply.

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:46.979
Adult mosquitoes can be captured in traps
placed over windows of huts.

00:05:46.979 --> 00:05:50.990
The study of these mosquitoes will show, among
other things, which species are the most important

00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:52.270
vectors in a given locality.

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:57.370
This is done by demonstrating sporozoites,
the infective stage of the malaria parasite,

00:05:57.370 --> 00:05:59.599
in the salivary glands of the mosquito.

00:05:59.599 --> 00:06:03.539
These glands are easily dissected from the
mosquito and when gently crushed under the

00:06:03.539 --> 00:06:08.340
cover slip, the sporozoites are expelled and
can be readily seen under the microscope.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:12.949
Only a small portion of the mosquitoes captured
are found infected.

00:06:12.949 --> 00:06:17.190
But in view of the vast numbers which feed
on unprotected individuals in the tropics,

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:21.300
even a small percentage of infected mosquitoes
is sufficient to maintain a high incidence

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:24.759
of infection in the population.

00:06:24.759 --> 00:06:28.860
The clinical diagnosis of malaria is frequently
difficult.

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:32.780
It may be confused with many diseases, both
cosmopolitian and tropical.

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:38.199
This is inevitable in view of the pathology
which consists mainly of mechanical interference

00:06:38.199 --> 00:06:42.060
with the vascular supply of many organs of
the body.

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:46.770
Among the tropical diseases with which it
may be confused are telehezar (??), amoebic

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:51.280
liver abscess, relapsing fever, and yellow
fever.

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:56.900
Among the cosmopolitian diseases, it may simulate
typhoid fever, tuberculosis, brucellosis,

00:06:56.900 --> 00:07:02.599
influenza, myelitis, and other septic conditions
as well as certain diseases of the central

00:07:02.599 --> 00:07:04.060
nervous system.

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:08.509
Great strides have been made in recent years
in developing new drugs for the suppression

00:07:08.509 --> 00:07:13.330
and treatment of malaria by testing thousands
of compounds against the malarias of lower

00:07:13.330 --> 00:07:15.349
animals, such as chicks.

00:07:15.349 --> 00:07:23.980
The drug being tested here is administered
orally to the infected chick.

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:28.130
Blood smears are prepared before and at intervals
after treatment.

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:32.259
The anti-malarial effect of the compound is
then determined by the presence or absence

00:07:32.259 --> 00:07:41.509
of the malaria parasite.

00:07:41.509 --> 00:07:46.370
An essential part of all research concerns
the publication of the results.

00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:51.069
Shown here is a monograph in which data on
nearly four thousand compounds are analyzed

00:07:51.069 --> 00:07:56.590
to show the relationship between chemical
structure and anti-malarial activity.

00:07:56.590 --> 00:08:01.720
The final evaluation of any anti-malarial
drug must of course be made against human

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:04.620
malaria in endemic areas.

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:10.030
[Onchocerciasis] Onchocerciasis is a form
of filariasis transmitted by gnats of the

00:08:10.030 --> 00:08:11.030
genus Simulium.

00:08:11.030 --> 00:08:16.570
The adult worms live in subcutaneous nodules,
while the larvae, or microfilari, migrate

00:08:16.570 --> 00:08:18.310
within the layers of the skin.

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:22.919
These microfilari frequently penetrate into
the structures of the eye so that a variable

00:08:22.919 --> 00:08:28.060
proportion of the population in the endemic
area suffers from partial or complete blindness.

00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:32.780
Blindness due to this disease usually affects
less than 1 percent of the population.

00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:38.480
However, in scattered focii, the incidence
may be extremely high.

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:43.250
The subcutaneous nodules, which contain the
coiled up adult worms, are the most characteristic

00:08:43.250 --> 00:08:45.630
lesions of onchocerciasis.

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:49.700
Ocular changes usually occur only after long
and repeated exposure.

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:54.130
These changes bear no relation to the anatomic
location of the nodules but rather are associated

00:08:54.130 --> 00:08:59.490
with the development of sensitivity to antigenic
substances produced by the parasite or its

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:01.090
product.

00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:06.690
The most widely distributed vector of onchocerciasis
in Africa is the gnat Simulium damnosum, which

00:09:06.690 --> 00:09:10.670
breeds in cataracts and waterfalls of rapidly
flowing streams where the larvae and pupae

00:09:10.670 --> 00:09:15.090
may be found attached to submerged stones
and vegetation.

00:09:15.090 --> 00:09:19.550
The other principle vector is Simulium [medei],
which is found primarily in East Africa and

00:09:19.550 --> 00:09:20.990
parts of the Congo.

00:09:20.990 --> 00:09:24.930
It was only after years of diligent search
that the larvae and pupae of this fly were

00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:31.630
found to live only on the carapace and legs
of a freshwater crab, potamon niloticus.

00:09:31.630 --> 00:09:35.920
The discovery of the oscology of the larval
and pupal stages of this gnat has greatly

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:41.230
simplified the control and eradication of
onchocerciasis in the areas in which Simulium

00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:43.120
[medei] is the sole vector.

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:47.850
The Simulia are daytime feeders which have
a painless bite and are not easily disturbed

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:49.700
after they have started feeding.

00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:53.160
The incidence of infection in these flies
is determined by searching for developing

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:58.230
stages of the worm onchocerca in nets captured
while feeding on natives serving as human

00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:00.860
bait.

00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:05.779
The ingested larvae, or microfilari, leave
the stomach of the fly and penetrate the thoracic

00:10:05.779 --> 00:10:09.560
muscles where they grow into the so-called
'sausage stage'.

00:10:09.560 --> 00:10:14.550
After six days or more of development, the
elongated infective larvae travel to the labium

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:15.550
of the fly.

00:10:15.550 --> 00:10:20.730
When the insect bites another person, the
larvae escape and penetrate into the skin.

00:10:20.730 --> 00:10:25.000
The microfilari live in the dermis of the
skin and can be demonstrated easily in skin

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:30.050
biopsies which consist of thin slices of skin
placed in drops of normal saline.

00:10:30.050 --> 00:10:36.680
This method is satisfactory only where onchocerca
is the only filarid encountered, since microfilari,

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:45.660
which are characteristically found in the
blood, occasionally occur in skin biopsies.

00:10:45.660 --> 00:10:50.130
All species of microfilari can be readily
demonstrated and identified in scarification

00:10:50.130 --> 00:10:56.060
smears which are prepared by making several
superficial incisions in the skin with a scalpel

00:10:56.060 --> 00:11:05.380
and squeezing out the blood and tissue juices.

00:11:05.380 --> 00:11:10.540
This material can be examined fresh for motile
micro filari, but must be examined as a thick

00:11:10.540 --> 00:11:14.320
blood smear stained with hematoxylin to determine
the species.

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:20.550
Wuchereria bancrofti microfilari have a sheath
and the nuclei extends farther forward than

00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:25.990
do those of the onchocerca volvulus which
have a somewhat bulbous head and no sheath.

00:11:25.990 --> 00:11:29.180
[Trypanosomiasis]
Trypanosomiasis is an acute and chronic disease

00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:33.180
produced by protozoan parasites of the genus
Trypanosoma.

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:39.300
American trypanosomiasis, or Chagas disease,
is caused by trypanosoma cruzi which is transmitted

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:41.279
by bugs of the genus Tryatoma.

00:11:41.279 --> 00:11:42.600
[Trypanosoma Cruzi]

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:47.680
African trypanosomiasis, or sleeping sickness,
is caused by trypanosoma gambiense in the

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:52.570
western and equatorial parts of Africa and
trypanosoma rhodesiense in the eastern part

00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:53.630
of the continent.

00:11:53.630 --> 00:11:54.699
[Trypanosoma Cruzi.

00:11:54.699 --> 00:11:55.779
Tryapanosoma Gambiense.

00:11:55.779 --> 00:11:56.860
Trypanosoma Rhodesiense]

00:11:56.860 --> 00:12:00.380
Both are transmitted by various species of
glossina or tsetse flies.

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:05.380
It is said that the economic future of equatorial
Africa depends largely upon the issue of the

00:12:05.380 --> 00:12:10.240
struggle of science against the scourge of
trypanosom diseases which affect both man

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:11.850
and domestic animals.

00:12:11.850 --> 00:12:17.230
The sleeping or somnolent stage of gambien
trypanosomiasis begins with apathy associated

00:12:17.230 --> 00:12:23.329
with tremors, shuffling gait, and incoordination
from which it proceeds to true coma and eventually

00:12:23.329 --> 00:12:24.329
death.

00:12:24.329 --> 00:12:29.029
The latter is usually due to malnutrition
or intercurrent infection.

00:12:29.029 --> 00:12:34.089
When the trypanosomes are ingested by the
tsetse fly, they undergo a cyclical development

00:12:34.089 --> 00:12:35.770
in the digestive tract.

00:12:35.770 --> 00:12:40.850
After 18-34 days, infective trypanosomes leave
the salivary ducts and enter the bite wound

00:12:40.850 --> 00:12:42.240
at the next feeding.

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:47.620
Glossina palpalis, the tsetse fly which transmits
Gambian sleeping sickness, frequents the dense

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:50.190
undergrowth bordering water courses.

00:12:50.190 --> 00:12:54.920
The pregnant female produces a single, large,
fully developed larvae at intervals of about

00:12:54.920 --> 00:12:56.440
ten days.

00:12:56.440 --> 00:13:01.670
The larva burrows into the ground to a depth
of about two inches and immediately pupates.

00:13:01.670 --> 00:13:06.010
The pupa hatches into an adult fly in three
to 12 weeks.

00:13:06.010 --> 00:13:10.759
Tsetse flies of the glossina morsitans group
which transmit Rhodesian sleeping sickness

00:13:10.759 --> 00:13:15.949
are less dependent upon water and are found
in wooded savanna country that provides moderate

00:13:15.949 --> 00:13:16.949
shade.

00:13:16.949 --> 00:13:21.620
Cover is more or less essential and suitable
breeding grounds largely determine the location

00:13:21.620 --> 00:13:26.329
of the fly belt.

00:13:26.329 --> 00:13:30.620
With drugs now available, it is possible to
cure the majority of sleeping sickness patients,

00:13:30.620 --> 00:13:35.160
provided they are treated before invasion
of the central nervous system by the parasite.

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:40.269
All individuals in an endemic area are examined
for evidence of the disease, such as enlarged

00:13:40.269 --> 00:13:46.660
posterior cervical lymph nodes, which is called
Winterbottom's sign.

00:13:46.660 --> 00:13:52.120
And for the presence of the parasite in blood
smears.

00:13:52.120 --> 00:14:01.940
Nodule aspiration is useful in diagnosing
the Gambian form of the disease.

00:14:01.940 --> 00:14:12.579
A study of the spinal fluid will determine
whether the central nervous system is involved.

00:14:12.579 --> 00:14:16.850
An increase in the amount of protein or in
the cell count indicates such involvement.

00:14:16.850 --> 00:14:21.579
In which event, the patient must be treated
with one of the more toxic, arsenical compounds

00:14:21.579 --> 00:14:23.660
such as tryparsamide or arsabal.

00:14:23.660 --> 00:14:28.759
Due to the rather ineffective methods of diagnosing
sleeping sickness, attempts are being made

00:14:28.759 --> 00:14:32.459
to develop tests which will detect the disease
in early stages.

00:14:32.459 --> 00:14:37.870
Fractions of protein derived from trypanosomes
are purified by various procedures including

00:14:37.870 --> 00:14:43.220
dialysis and then concentrated by evaporization
and myopalization.

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:47.660
Drying is completed under vacuum while the
material is still frozen.

00:14:47.660 --> 00:14:58.889
The resulting product is then used in various
types of serologic tests.

00:14:58.889 --> 00:15:04.370
Information gleaned from basic research on
the physiology and metabolism of the trypanosome

00:15:04.370 --> 00:15:08.069
contributes to knowledge of the parasite and
may lead to the development of a drug that

00:15:08.069 --> 00:15:12.430
will destroy them regardless of their location
in the human body.

00:15:12.430 --> 00:15:18.327
[Comparative Studies on the metabolism of
the culture form and bloodstream form of Trypanosoma

00:15:18.327 --> 00:15:19.327
Gambiense]

00:15:19.327 --> 00:15:20.327
[Schistosomiasis]
Schistosomiasis, a chronic disease caused

00:15:20.327 --> 00:15:24.709
by blood flukes of the genus Schistosoma,
is characterized by inflammatory and proliferative

00:15:24.709 --> 00:15:29.310
lesions of the liver, genital urinary, and
lower intestinal tracts.

00:15:29.310 --> 00:15:33.900
Several species of schistosomes are capable
of infecting man but only three are important

00:15:33.900 --> 00:15:40.190
clinically: Schistosoma mansoni and the more
virulent Schistosoma japonicum primarily involve

00:15:40.190 --> 00:15:45.550
the intestinal tract, While Schistosoma hematobium
affects the genital urinary tract.

00:15:45.550 --> 00:15:46.550
[Schistosoma mansoni.

00:15:46.550 --> 00:15:47.550
Schistosoma japonicum.

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:48.550
Schistosoma hematobium.]

00:15:48.550 --> 00:15:50.319
Schistosomiasis is one of the most widespread
and serious of all the diseases produced by

00:15:50.319 --> 00:15:52.440
animal parasites.

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:56.569
The disease is estimated to affect 115 million
people in the world and seems to be spreading

00:15:56.569 --> 00:15:58.720
into near-eastern Brazil.

00:15:58.720 --> 00:16:02.730
Shown here is a district health clinic in
the Liberian hinterland staffed by a U.S.

00:16:02.730 --> 00:16:06.540
Public Health Service physician under the
foreign technical assistance program.

00:16:06.540 --> 00:16:11.120
Nearly half of the patients attending this
clinic have urinary schistosomiasis.

00:16:11.120 --> 00:16:17.199
Symptoms of this infection are caused by small
ulcers in the bladder and consist of hematuria,

00:16:17.199 --> 00:16:21.450
burning on urination, and super-pubic or perineal
pain.

00:16:21.450 --> 00:16:26.460
The prevalence of schistosomiasis varies considerably
from place to place, even in the same endemic

00:16:26.460 --> 00:16:31.410
area, and hence surveys are necessary to evaluate
its local importance.

00:16:31.410 --> 00:16:36.850
The ova of schistosoma hematoibum in the urine
are concentrated by centrifugation.

00:16:36.850 --> 00:16:42.940
The supernatant is decanted and the sediment
is examined under the microscope for the eggs.

00:16:42.940 --> 00:16:48.630
When the schistosome egg reaches fresh water,
a ciliated larva, or miracidium, is free and

00:16:48.630 --> 00:16:53.350
must find and penetrate a suitable snail host
in order to continue the life cycle.

00:16:53.350 --> 00:16:59.649
Only a few species of snails are capable of
serving as intermediate hosts for the schistosomes.

00:16:59.649 --> 00:17:04.910
In the snail, the parasite undergoes a two-phase
process of development ending in four to seven

00:17:04.910 --> 00:17:11.640
weeks with the production of tens of thousands
of free swimming, infective forms called cercariae.

00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:15.970
When an individual wades or works in infested
waters, the organisms become attached to the

00:17:15.970 --> 00:17:19.630
exposed skin and penetrate within a few minutes.

00:17:19.630 --> 00:17:24.920
Slow-moving streams clogged with vegetation
and high organic content are particularly

00:17:24.920 --> 00:17:28.220
favorable habitats for the snails which transmit
Schistosomiasis.

00:17:28.220 --> 00:17:33.850
In such advantageous surroundings, these snails
frequently are found in enormous numbers.

00:17:33.850 --> 00:17:38.670
Man himself contributes to their welfare and
to the spread of the disease when he disturbs

00:17:38.670 --> 00:17:43.890
natural conditions and constructs new ponds,
alters the course of streams, or introduces

00:17:43.890 --> 00:17:45.660
irrigation systems.

00:17:45.660 --> 00:17:50.001
Colonies of snails that serve as vectors of
schistosomiasis in many parts of the world

00:17:50.001 --> 00:17:54.480
are maintained at the National Institutes
of Health and are utilized in research on

00:17:54.480 --> 00:18:01.830
prevention and control of this disease.

00:18:01.830 --> 00:18:06.299
The molluscicide effect of numerous compounds
has been determined in the laboratory by mixing

00:18:06.299 --> 00:18:11.159
various concentrations of the substance being
tested in small aquaria.

00:18:11.159 --> 00:18:16.170
The vector snails are then added to the treated
water and after a stated interval of time,

00:18:16.170 --> 00:18:20.669
the snails are removed and the lethal action
of the compound is determined by noting the

00:18:20.669 --> 00:18:23.470
proportion of dead and live snails.

00:18:23.470 --> 00:18:28.929
Using this or some other techniques, it has
been shown that sodium pentachlorophenate

00:18:28.929 --> 00:18:34.429
is an effective molluscicide, and numerous
field tests have been made with this compound.

00:18:34.429 --> 00:18:39.210
Shown here is a report on an experiment to
compare the effect of this chemical on different

00:18:39.210 --> 00:18:45.080
laboratory strains of the same vector species.

00:18:45.080 --> 00:18:48.850
Studies to determine the mechanism of action
of sodium pentaclorophenate have been made

00:18:48.850 --> 00:18:50.669
on various species of snails.

00:18:50.669 --> 00:18:56.000
Shown here is an experiment in which a suspension
of cells is prepared by convoluting the liver

00:18:56.000 --> 00:19:01.710
of a snail followed by differential centrifugation.

00:19:01.710 --> 00:19:06.570
Various concentrations of a molluscicide are
added to this suspension and the effect on

00:19:06.570 --> 00:19:09.730
the metabolism on the cell is observed.

00:19:09.730 --> 00:19:16.110
These studies indicate that the molluscicidal
property of sodium pentachlorophenate is due

00:19:16.110 --> 00:19:21.030
at least in part to its ability to inactivate
certain enzyme systems and thus prevent the

00:19:21.030 --> 00:19:27.650
cell from utilizing the energy available from
the metabolic breakdown of nutriments.

00:19:27.650 --> 00:19:33.780
Comparative studies on the physiology and
metabolism of the same species of vector snails

00:19:33.780 --> 00:19:39.230
from different geographical areas, such as
the anaerobic carbon dioxide production experiment

00:19:39.230 --> 00:19:44.450
shown here, have demonstrated much differences
in what appear to be identical snails.

00:19:44.450 --> 00:19:50.340
This helps to explain why snails in different
areas do not always react similarly to identical

00:19:50.340 --> 00:19:51.559
control measures.

00:19:51.559 --> 00:19:55.570
The schistosomacidal activity of numerous
drugs has been tested in mice.

00:19:55.570 --> 00:20:02.159
These animals have been infected by inserting
their tails into waters containing live cercariae.

00:20:02.159 --> 00:20:06.710
After the infection is developed the mice
were treated with the test compound, and after

00:20:06.710 --> 00:20:11.679
suitable intervals are autopsied to determined
the schistosomacidal effect of the drug.

00:20:11.679 --> 00:20:17.919
Untreated mice, or those treated with ineffective
compounds, have adult worms in their messentaric

00:20:17.919 --> 00:20:22.840
and portal veins and evidence of innumerable
eggs in the liver.

00:20:22.840 --> 00:20:28.630
Mice treated with effective drugs have few
eggs in the liver and show remains of dead

00:20:28.630 --> 00:20:30.490
worms in the liver tissue.

00:20:30.490 --> 00:20:35.390
Furthermore, all adult worms have disappeared
from the messentaric and portal veins.

00:20:35.390 --> 00:20:42.081
In this experiment, antibiotic complexes of
antimony trichloride were found to be active

00:20:42.081 --> 00:20:44.940
orally against Schistosoma mansoni.

00:20:44.940 --> 00:20:49.010
But no more active than plain antimony trichloride
or tartar emetic.

00:20:49.010 --> 00:20:54.530
It is hoped that eventually an effective and
nontoxic drug will be found to aid in the

00:20:54.530 --> 00:21:00.000
control of what the World Health Organization
considers to be one of the world's most important

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:01.000
health problems.

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:04.990
During the past half-century, great strides
have been made in our knowledge of tropical

00:21:04.990 --> 00:21:06.050
diseases.

00:21:06.050 --> 00:21:10.760
However, only a few of these diseases have
been adequately controlled.

00:21:10.760 --> 00:21:15.039
Because of their public health and economic
importance, they must be studied more intensively

00:21:15.039 --> 00:21:17.640
both in the laboratory and in the field.

00:21:17.640 --> 00:21:22.850
If these maladies can be further checked and
ultimately eradicated, Africa need no longer

00:21:22.850 --> 00:21:25.840
be referred to as the dark continent.

00:21:25.840 --> 00:21:30.480
Equal benefits will rebound to other parts
of the globe where many of these scourges

00:21:30.480 --> 00:21:32.430
also take their toll.

00:21:32.430 --> 00:21:33.530
[The End]

00:21:33.530 --> 00:21:40.130
